---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<Base
  title="Offdesk Morning Review Workflow - Forager"
  description="Prepare configured Offdesk tasks, inspect logs and result artifacts, and record review decisions before choosing the next step."
>
  <Nav />

  <article class="pt-32 pb-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-content">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">Offdesk Morning Review Workflow</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          Forager provides task queues, execution approvals and review records for configured commands. Before leaving a task unattended, check its runner, scope and stop conditions. On return, inspect the artifacts it actually produced.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>The Loop</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Ondesk planning:</strong> create focused sessions, use separate branch checkouts, and select tasks with an explicit scope, runner and stop conditions.</li>
          <li><strong>Offdesk execution:</strong> queue durable tasks behind approval gates, provider fallback checks, recovery records, and audit trails.</li>
          <li><strong>Morning review:</strong> inspect task status, background probes, debug bundles, maintenance reports, Council decisions, result artifacts, implementation packet coverage, and closeout needs.</li>
          <li><strong>Closeout:</strong> generate the dry-run cleanup plan, commercial review packet, and Ondesk return package before resuming hands-on work.</li>
          <li><strong>Knowledge promotion:</strong> promote adaptive wiki candidates only after reviewing evidence, counterexamples, scope, and freshness.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What to Check First</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the high-signal surfaces before opening individual agent terminals:
        </p>
        <pre><code>forager status
forager offdesk tasks --json
forager offdesk pending --json
forager offdesk maintenance-report --json
forager offdesk closeout --dry-run</code></pre>
        <p>
          These commands answer the operational questions: what ran, what is waiting, what failed, what needs recovery, what requires explicit approval, and what must be cleaned up before Ondesk return.
        </p>
        <p>
          The TUI morning-review card and status bar now use the same <code>next_safe_actions</code> contract as <code>forager status</code>, <code>offdesk pending</code>, <code>offdesk poll</code>, and <code>offdesk maintenance-report</code>. Treat the first action as the next bounded operator step, not as broad permission to clean up files, promote wiki entries, or accept results.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Implementation Packet Coverage</h2>
        <p>
          If the overnight task started from an implementation packet, review whether the original goal, work slices, validation items, and expected artifacts came back as completed, deferred, missing, or drifted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Runner-generated deferred work-slice receipts mean the supervised run reached a terminal result and left a result artifact. They are not acceptance. They are the cue for Ondesk review, worker-authored receipt follow-up, or a narrower recovery task.
        </p>
        <p>
          Treat worker reports as claims. For code or artifact-producing work, compare the claim with diffs, changed files, test output, logs, and generated artifacts before accepting the result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Closeout Review</h2>
        <p>
          The TUI marks completed tasks that still need closeout. Treat that signal as mandatory before resuming normal Ondesk work:
        </p>
        <pre><code>forager offdesk closeout --project-key &lt;project&gt; --dry-run</code></pre>
        <p>
          Closeout writes a plan, cleanup manifest, commercial review packet, and return package. It does not move, delete, archive, or mutate project files.
        </p>
        <pre><code>forager offdesk closeout-review --closeout-id &lt;closeout-id&gt; --verdict approved</code></pre>
        <p>
          The review record stores the submitted verdict. It does not establish how thoroughly the packet was inspected or whether the result is correct. Packet generation alone does not approve file cleanup.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the <a href={sitePath('/guides/offdesk-closeout/')}>Offdesk Closeout Guide</a> when file cleanup, archive candidates, or handoff context are part of the return path.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Adaptive Wiki Review</h2>
        <p>
          Overnight runs may create wiki candidates or provisional trial entries. They should not silently become future instructions. Review them as evidence-backed proposals:
        </p>
        <pre><code>forager offdesk wiki candidates --json
forager offdesk wiki lint --json
forager offdesk wiki review --json
forager offdesk wiki export-markdown --output ./adaptive-wiki-review</code></pre>
        <p>
          The canonical source remains the Forager profile state. Markdown exports are human review snapshots, not writable truth.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Decision Rules</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Compare completion claims with the task's requirements and checks. A terminal status or result file alone does not establish success.</li>
          <li>Do not let wiki context change commands, workdirs, provider choices, models, or approvals.</li>
          <li>Use Council output as a read-only recommendation, not as permission to mutate state.</li>
          <li>Do not move, delete, or archive closeout candidates without commercial review and explicit human approval.</li>
          <li>Convert failures into recovery tasks, maintenance requests, or revised plans instead of silent retries.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Prepare the Next Run</h2>
        <p>
          A good night run starts from the morning review. Pick one narrow goal, define stop conditions, choose the agent modes involved, and queue only the work that has the required approvals and evidence boundaries.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
